import Foundation

/// The words of "How it works" and "What's new". Every release adds its entry at the top of `news`
/// and changes the parts of `guide` that it touched.
enum Guide {
    struct Part: Identifiable {
        let title: String
        let text: [String]
        var id: String { title }
    }

    struct Release: Identifiable {
        let version: String
        let date: String
        let items: [String]
        var id: String { version }
    }

    static let guide: [Part] = [
        Part(title: "What this app is", text: [
            "AMS Reading Companion is one shelf for every book you have in three stores: Rakuten Kobo, Apple Books and Amazon Kindle. Your own files (EPUB and PDF) stand on it too.",
            "It shows what you own and where. It stops you buying a book twice. It helps you move books that have no lock.",
            "It never takes a store's lock off a book. A locked book still opens only in its own store's app or reader.",
            "On the shelf: bought ebooks, audiobooks and your own files. Free samples are left out.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Where the lists come from", text: [
            "The Mac collects the lists. It does so each time the app opens there, and when you press Refresh in Settings.",
            "iPhone and iPad cannot read the stores. They show the same shelf through iCloud.",
            "So after you buy a book, open the store's app on the Mac once so that it syncs. Then open AMS Reading Companion on the Mac.",
            "Settings shows for each store how many books were found and when.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Apple Books", text: [
            "The whole list is read from the Books app on your Mac: ebooks, audiobooks and PDFs.",
            "This list is the truth. A book that leaves it leaves your shelf at the next read.",
            "If the list comes back empty (for example when you are signed out), nothing is removed.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Kobo", text: [
            "The whole list is read from Kobo's app on your Mac. Sign in there once and let it sync.",
            "This list is the truth too. A book that leaves it leaves your shelf at the next read. An empty list removes nothing.",
            "This part has not yet been checked against your real Kobo list.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Kindle", text: [
            "The Kindle app on the Mac keeps only a small list from its latest sync, not your whole library. Built-in dictionaries and entries without a purchase day are left out. On 17 November 2026 that left 1 book: the one you bought. So Kindle books come in three ways.",
            "One: new purchases are picked up from the Kindle app on the Mac after it has synced.",
            "Two: a one-time list of your whole Kindle library. Take it in under Settings › Import Kindle list. It can be a CSV or JSON file with title, author and Amazon's book number for each book.",
            "Three: by hand with Add a book.",
            "The list file can now be Amazon's page saved as a PDF. Amazon does not let a helper read the page, so you do it in your own browser: sign in at Amazon, open “Manage Your Content and Devices” and choose Books. Sort by Acquired. Save the page as a PDF (print, then Save as PDF). In AMS Reading Companion open Settings › Import Kindle list and pick that PDF.",
            "Amazon shows only part of your list on a page, for example 25 of 33 books. The app tells you so. Go to Amazon's next page, save it as a PDF too and import it. Books that Amazon marks READ come in as Finished. A book that is already on your shelf with the same title is not added a second time.",
            "Kindle books are never removed by themselves, because the Mac's Kindle list is never complete. The one exception: if a store's list later marks a book on your shelf as a free sample or a built-in item, it leaves the shelf at the next read. You can also remove a book yourself on its page.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "One book, several stores", text: [
            "The same title and author in two stores is one book with two store marks.",
            "Matching ignores capital letters, accents, a subtitle after a colon and a leading “The”. “Herbert, Frank” counts as “Frank Herbert”.",
            "When you own the same kind twice (two ebooks, or two audiobooks) the book says “Owned twice”.",
            "An ebook and its audiobook are not “twice”.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The shelves", text: [
            "The main screen is a stack of shelves. Each has a heading in its own colour and a row of covers that scrolls sideways. A book can stand on several shelves. Empty shelves are left out, except the Wish list.",
            "The usual order from the top: Reading now, Up next, your own shelves (see The big filter), Not read yet, each series, Set aside, Lock-free, Audiobooks, Bought twice, Finished, Gave up, Wish list. You can hide shelves and change the order (see Choose your shelves).",
            "Reading now (purple): the books you opened lately. A book you set to Reading yourself always stays here.",
            "Up next (indigo): the books you chose to read next, in your order, with the count, for example “Up next · 3”. See Up next.",
            "Not read yet (red): newest purchase first, with the count, for example “Not read yet · 84”. Books you started are not here any more.",
            "One shelf for each series of which you own two books or more (green), in series order. The note says “3 of 7 · missing 2 and 4” when a public book catalogue knows how long the series is. When it does not, the note says “3 owned · missing 2”, and gaps are then known only below the highest number you own. You can also type the length yourself (see Series length by hand).",
            "Set aside (brown): books you started but have not opened in the 90 days before your latest reading. Before, they stood under Not read yet. Reading now and Set aside never hold the same book.",
            "Lock-free (teal). Audiobooks (amber).",
            "Bought twice (pink): every book you own to read, or to listen to, in two stores, for example Kindle and Apple Books. Your own files do not count.",
            "Finished (grey): most recent first. Gave up (grey): books you started and gave up on. Wish list (blue): shown even when empty, unless you hide it.",
            "Your own shelves (sea blue) are filters you kept. Each shows its name and how many books fit, for example “Summer reads · 12”.",
            "A started book carries a thin bar along the bottom of its cover that shows how far you have read. It is there only for a book that is Reading now or Set aside, and only when the store reports the progress (Apple Books and Kobo do, Kindle does not). The Mac reads it with the lists.",
            "Tap a shelf's heading to see all its books in a grid. There you can narrow the books down and put them in another order (see Sort and filter). On a series shelf’s page, “All parts” opens the series’ page (see The series’ page). On a shelf of your own, “Change” changes the filter behind it.",
            "On iPhone, a tap on a cover opens the book's page and Back returns to the shelves. On iPad and Mac with a wide window the book shows beside the shelves (see On iPad and Mac).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "On iPad and Mac", text: [
            "When the window is wide (760 points or more), the screen has two parts side by side. Left: the shelves, with the What next? card, the coloured headings, the rows of covers and the buttons at the top (including the button for Your reading). On the Mac the filter button is among the buttons in the top bar; on iPad and iPhone it stands at the top left. The pen for a new note stands on the “Reading notes” line. Right: the page of the book you tapped.",
            "Tap a cover and the right part shows that book. Tap another cover and it changes. The chosen cover has a purple frame. You never leave the shelves.",
            "When the app opens, the first book of Reading now is shown on the right. If there is none, the right part says “Tap a cover and the book shows here.”",
            "Tap a shelf's heading and that shelf's page (the grid with sort and filter) opens on the left. A tap on a cover there also shows the book on the right. “Next in the series” on the right shows the next book on the right.",
            "A narrow window (under 760 points, such as a small Mac window or an iPad in a narrow split) works like the iPhone: a tap on a cover opens the book's page, and Back returns.",
            "Search, Add a book, Scan, Settings, the filter and the notebook open as pages over the screen. The author’s page, the series’ page and the results of a filter open on the left, in the shelves’ own stack. A book you open from Search opens inside the Search page.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "What next?", text: [
            "A card above the shelves gives one idea for your next book. It looks at books you own and have not started.",
            "First ideas: the books on your Up next list, in your order. The first one says “First on your Up next list”. Then the next book of a series whose earlier book you finished, when you own it. Then your newest book. Then the ones that have waited longest, with the month you bought them.",
            "Tap the card to open the book. “Another” shows the next idea. The card is left out when there is no idea.",
            "“Surprise me” on the card picks a book by chance among the books you own and have not started. It says “A surprise from the books you have not started”. It does not show the same book twice in a row when there is another. Press “Another” to go back to the ideas.",
            "Under the card, when you have written reading notes, a line says “Reading notes · 7”. A tap opens the notebook (see Reading notes).",
            "The Home Screen widget “Reading” shows one of these ideas too (see The Home Screen widget).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Up next", text: [
            "Up next is your own list of books to read next.",
            "On the page of a book you own and have not started, tap “Add to Up next”. The button then reads “Take off Up next”.",
            "The main screen has a shelf “Up next · 3” (indigo) right under Reading now. It is left out when the list is empty.",
            "Tap the shelf's heading to open its page. There “Change order” opens a page where you move books up or down or take them off. Cancel closes it without saving. Save keeps your order.",
            "What next? offers the Up next books first, in your order.",
            "A book you start or finish leaves the list by itself.",
            "The list travels between your devices. If two devices changed it, the newer list wins, whole. The two lists are not mixed.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Your reading", text: [
            "A small button in the top bar of the main screen opens “Your reading”. It has two parts: “Year” and “Numbers”.",
            "Year: for each year a heading such as “2026 · 8 books”, and under it the books you finished that year, the latest first, with your stars and the day you finished them.",
            "“Set goal” asks how many books you want to finish this year. Then a line shows how you stand: “8 of 24 · 2 behind”, “ahead”, “on track”, or “goal reached”. A goal holds for the following years until you change it. Empty or 0 means no goal. Cancel closes without saving, Save keeps it.",
            "The finishing day: today when you mark a book Finished. When a store reports a book as finished, the day the store last saw it open. Books you finished before version 0.5 count on the day they were last opened. On a finished book's page you can correct the day.",
            "Finished books whose day nobody knows stand under “Day not known”.",
            "Numbers: how many books you own, how many wishes, how many are lock-free, how many you bought twice. Then bars by store, by kind (ebooks, audiobooks, PDFs), by reading status, by language, books bought per year and books finished per year. Last, your average stars.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Language", text: [
            "Every book now carries its language. Apple Books and Kobo tell it for most books. Kindle does not.",
            "Where no store tells, the app makes a careful guess from the title. It guesses only Swedish, German or English, and only when it is sure. A title shorter than 12 letters gets no guess. The letter å means Swedish, the letter ß means German.",
            "A book's page shows “Language” and lets you change it. What you set by hand is never overwritten.",
            "On a shelf's page the sort and filter control has a choice “Language”. The Numbers part counts books for each language.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The Home Screen widget", text: [
            "On iPhone and iPad you can add the widget “Reading” to the Home Screen. It is not on the Mac.",
            "Small: the book you are reading now. Medium: up to two books you are reading now and one “What next?” idea with its reason.",
            "A tap opens that book's page in the app.",
            "The widget refreshes when the shelf changes, and at least every 6 hours. It fetches the covers itself. A book without a cover shows the same coloured title tile as in the app.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Search from your device", text: [
            "The search of your iPhone, iPad or Mac finds your books. Type a title or an author.",
            "The result says “You own this · Kobo, Kindle” (with your stores) or “On your wish list”. A tap opens the book's page in the app.",
            "The list the device searches is kept on the device only. Nothing is sent anywhere.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The dots and marks", text: [
            "Under each cover stand small coloured dots for its stores: Kobo red-orange, Kindle blue, Apple Books grey, own file teal.",
            "An open-lock mark means the book has no lock. A headphones mark means an audiobook.",
            "A book without a cover picture shows a coloured tile with its title.",
            "Books that came without a cover (Kindle books from a list file, books you added by hand, wishes) get one if a public book catalogue knows it. The app asks once per book, by title and author. When nothing fits, the coloured title tile stays.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Search: do I own it?", text: [
            "Tap the search button and type a title, an author, a series, a word from your own note or one of your tags.",
            "Each match shows the book with its stores. Use this before you buy.",
            "If nothing matches, the app says “Not on your shelf” and offers a button to add what you typed.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Add a book", text: [
            "Tap the add button in the toolbar. On iPhone and iPad it offers “Add a book” and “Scan a barcode” (see Scan a barcode). On the Mac it adds a book by hand. Type the title and the author, and choose where the book is: Wish list (the usual choice), Kindle, Kobo, Apple Books or Own file.",
            "If you already own that title, the page tells you so and names the store before you save.",
            "Cancel closes the page without saving. On the Mac, Escape is Cancel.",
            "A book you added by hand to a store is replaced by the store's own entry when the store's list later brings the same title.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Scan a barcode", text: [
            "On iPhone and iPad, tap the add button and choose “Scan a barcode”. The camera reads the barcode of a printed book. Or type the 10 or 13 digits under the barcode and tap Look up.",
            "The app asks public book catalogues which book the number belongs to. It then says “You own this” with the stores, “Already on your wish list”, or “Not on your shelf” with a button “Add to wish list”.",
            "If the catalogues do not know the number, the app says so and offers “Add by hand”. Many books are known; some are not.",
            "The camera has not been tried on a real iPhone yet. On the Mac there is no scanning: the add button adds a book by hand.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Share: do I own it?", text: [
            "On iPhone and iPad, when you look at a book's page in Safari (a shop, Goodreads and so on), tap Share and choose AMS Reading.",
            "The app reads the page's title and compares it with your shelf. It says “You own this” with the store, or “Already on your wish list”, or “Not on your shelf”. In the last case it shows the title and the author it read from the page: correct them if needed, then press Save to wish list, or Cancel to add nothing.",
            "If it says to open AMS Reading once, do that and try again. The wish you add from Share arrives on your shelf the next time you open the app.",
            "The whole title must be the same: owning the first book of a series does not make the second one “owned”. A page title is not always clear, so an answer can still be wrong now and then.",
            "This is not on the Mac. It has not been tried on a real iPhone yet.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "A book's page", text: [
            "On iPhone, and in a narrow window, you reach this page with a tap on a cover. In a wide window on iPad or Mac it shows on the right, beside the shelves.",
            "The page shows the cover, the title, the author, the series and number, the language, each store the book is in, and when you bought it.",
            "Tap the author’s name to see every book of that author (see The author’s page). Tap the series line to see every part of the series (see The series’ page). This works on iPhone, iPad and Mac, but not on a book that you opened from Search.",
            "On a book you have started, the page says how far you have read, for example “42 % read”, when the store reports it.",
            "On a book you own and have not started, “Add to Up next” puts it on your Up next list. “Take off Up next” takes it off.",
            "“Language” shows the book's language and lets you change it. What you set by hand is kept.",
            "On a finished book you can correct the day you finished it.",
            "Reading status: Not read, Reading or Finished. It is first taken from the store: Apple Books and Kobo report your progress, Kindle does not. Once you set it by hand, your choice stays.",
            "On the page of a book you started, “Gave up on it” moves it from Set aside (or Reading now) to Gave up. “Pick it up again” brings it back.",
            "One Open button for each store starts that store's app at the book. If the app cannot jump to the book, it opens the store's app and you find the book there. Opening is known to work for Kindle on the Mac. Apple Books, Kobo and opening from iPhone or iPad have not been tried yet.",
            "Further down: your tags (see Tags), your reading notes (see Reading notes) and, when a catalogue knows it, what the book is about (see What it is about).",
            "At the bottom, “Correct title, author or series” (see Correct a book).",
            "Remove from shelf asks first. It is there for wishes, books you added by hand and Kindle books. An Apple Books or Kobo book would come back at the next read, so it has no remove button.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Your stars and note", text: [
            "Every book's page has “Your stars and note”. Tap Edit to give 1 to 5 stars and write one line. Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps it.",
            "Search also finds words in your notes.",
            "This is the one line about the whole book. For notes while you read, with quotes, thoughts and pages, see Reading notes.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Next in the series", text: [
            "When a book has a series and a number, its page says what comes next.",
            "If you own the next book, you see its cover and name, whether you have read it, and a tap opens it.",
            "If you do not own it but a public catalogue knows its name, you see the name and a button “Add to wish list”.",
            "If nothing is known but the number, you see “Book 4” and that it is not on your shelf. The last book of a series has no “next”.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Sort and filter", text: [
            "Tap a shelf's heading. At the top of the grid are small menus: one store (or all), to read or to listen (or both), one language (or all), and the order: shelf order, by title, by author, newest first.",
            "A line such as “Showing 12 of 340.” tells you how many books fit. The menus start again each time you open a shelf.",
            "To narrow down the whole shelf at once, use the funnel button on the main screen (see The big filter).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The big filter and your own shelves", text: [
            "The funnel button on the main screen narrows the whole shelf down on one page. It stands at the top left on iPhone and iPad, and in the top bar on the Mac.",
            "The page has groups of choices: Store, Kind, Language, Status, Your stars, Bought, Finished, Tags and Lock-free. Tap what you want. A group shows only what you have: a store you own nothing in is left out, Tags appears when you have tags, and Lock-free when you have a lock-free book.",
            "Inside one group, one choice is enough: Kobo and Kindle means a book in Kobo or in Kindle. Across groups, all groups must fit: Kobo and To read means a book that is in Kobo and is an ebook. A group with nothing chosen does not narrow.",
            "Your stars offers “3 or more”, “4 or more”, “5” and “None yet”. Bought offers your three latest years and “Earlier”. Finished offers your five latest years.",
            "Status has Not read, Reading, Set aside, Finished, Gave up and Wish. Wishes only come along when you choose Wish. Without it the filter looks at the books you own.",
            "The big button at the bottom counts as you tap: “Show 42 books”. “Clear” empties all choices. When no book fits, the button says “No book fits”.",
            "The result is a page with the books as covers, the choices in words, and how many books fit. “Change” goes back to the choices. “Keep” makes the filter a shelf of your own.",
            "Keep a filter as a shelf: name it (the page suggests the choices as a name) and press Save. The shelf (sea blue) stands on the main screen, near the top, and is always up to date: a new book that fits comes in by itself. “Keep as a shelf” is also on the filter page itself.",
            "On the page of a shelf of your own, “Change” opens its choices. You can change the name and the choices, and Save keeps them. “Remove this shelf” asks first. The books stay on your shelf; only the shelf goes.",
            "When no book fits a shelf of yours now, it says so. You can hide it or move it like any shelf (see Choose your shelves).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Tags", text: [
            "Tags are your own words for a book, for example “travel” or “to give away”.",
            "On a book’s page, “Add” (later “Edit”) under Tags opens a page. Tap a tag you already use to put it on this book, or type a new one and press Add. Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps the tags.",
            "A tag has at most 30 letters. Two spellings that differ only in capital letters or accents are one tag.",
            "Tap a tag on a book’s page to see every book that carries it.",
            "Search finds tags. The big filter has them under Tags.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Reading notes", text: [
            "Reading notes are for what you meet while you read: a quote, a thought, a to do, a word to look up. They are not the same as “Your stars and note”, the one line about the whole book.",
            "The pen button at the right end of the “Reading notes” line on the main screen (under What next?) starts a note for the book you are reading now. If you read no book, it takes the first book on your Up next list, or else your first book. You can change the book on the note’s page by tapping its line. If there is no book at all, the pen opens the notebook.",
            "The note’s page has the kinds on top: Quote, Thought, To do and Word to look up, with a small coloured dot each. Choose one. “Your own…” opens the kinds (see below). Then write.",
            "Three ways to write. Type: tap in the field and type. Speak: the device asks once for the microphone and for speech. Then speak, and press Stop. It listens in the book’s language (Swedish, German or English) or in the device’s own language, and does it on the device itself where it can. Photo of the page: on iPhone and iPad the camera takes a picture of the page; on the Mac you choose a picture file. The app reads the lines. You tick the ones to keep (“All” and “None” help), and “Use 3 lines” puts them in the note. With nothing ticked it uses all lines.",
            "A field “Page (if you like)” takes a page or a place, for example 41 or “chapter 3”. A note can have words and a page, and you can mix typing, speaking and photos in one note.",
            "Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps the note.",
            "Your notes show on the book’s page under “Reading notes · 3”, with “New note”. They also stand in the notebook: tap the line “Reading notes · 7” under What next? on the main screen. It holds every note, the newest first. The kinds on top (with how many notes each) narrow it down; “All” shows all again. “New note” starts one.",
            "Tap a note to change it, share it or remove it. Remove note asks first. You can also move a note to another book there.",
            "Share sends one note to any app, for example PARAGON. A quote goes in quotation marks, and below it stands the kind, the book, the author and the page.",
            "“Send all notes as one page” at the bottom of a book’s notes sends every note of that book as one tidy page, kind by kind, the oldest first. Quotes stand as quotes. A notes app shows the headings as headings.",
            "Your own kinds: Settings › Kinds of notes, or “Your own…” on the note’s page. There you add a kind with its name, rename any kind by typing over its name, or remove it with the red mark (it asks first). The four start kinds can be renamed or removed too. The notes of a removed kind stay and stand under “Other”. The last kind that is left cannot be removed, because a note needs a kind.",
            "When a book leaves your shelf, its notes are hidden with it.",
            "Not yet tried on a real device: Speak, Photo of the page, and sharing a note to another app.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The author’s page", text: [
            "On a book’s page, tap the author’s name. The page shows all the books of that author that you own, and the ones on your wish list. A line says how many: for example “5 books · 4 on your shelf · 1 wished · 2 finished”.",
            "Books in a series come first, in the series’ order, then the others by title. Wishes stand last, under “Wish list”.",
            "Each row has the cover, the title, the series and number, the status and the store marks. A tap opens the book. Back returns.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The series’ page", text: [
            "On a book’s page, tap the series line. Or, on a series shelf’s page, tap “All parts”. The page shows every part of the series in order, with its number.",
            "Owned parts show their status (Not read, Reading, Finished) and a tick. Wished parts say “On your wish list”. Missing parts show the name a public catalogue knows, or “Title not known”.",
            "A line on top counts them: “3 of 7 owned · 1 finished · 1 wished · 3 missing”.",
            "A missing part has a button “Wish”. One tap puts it on your wish list, in its place in the series. When nobody knows its title, it is wished as, for example, “Harbour Tales 3”.",
            "How long the series is comes from what a catalogue knows, or from the length you typed. “Set length” is on this page too (see Series length by hand). When neither is known, the page goes as far as the highest number you have.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Choose your shelves", text: [
            "Settings › Choose your shelves lists every shelf of the main screen, with how many books it has now (“empty now” when none).",
            "Tap the mark in front of a shelf to show it or hide it. The arrows move a shelf up or down. All series shelves move together as one line, “Series”.",
            "“Back to the usual order” shows every shelf again in the order of the start. Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps your choice and order.",
            "A hidden shelf keeps its books. It is only not shown on the main screen. The Wish list can be hidden too.",
            "A shelf of your own that you keep later stands near the top, until you move it.",
            "The choice and order travel between your devices. If two devices changed them, the newer one wins whole.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "What changed", text: [
            "Each time the Mac reads a store’s list, the app notes which books came and which left. Settings › What changed shows them, the newest read first, for example “Kobo · 18 November 2026”, with a plus before each book that came and a minus before each book that left.",
            "A read names at most 40 books; for more it says “and 12 more came”. The last 40 reads are kept.",
            "A small line at the very bottom of the main screen shows the latest read, for example “Kobo: 3 came, 1 left · 18 Nov”. A tap opens the page.",
            "A read that changed no book is not noted. A store’s very first read is not noted either, because it brings everything. Reads are noted from version 0.6 on. A Kindle list file you import by hand is not noted.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Correct a book", text: [
            "At the bottom of a book’s page, “Correct title, author or series” opens a page with four fields: Title, Author, Series and Number in the series.",
            "Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps it, and needs a title. An empty Series takes the book out of its series.",
            "After you save, the store’s list no longer changes the title, the author or the series of this book. The button then says “(corrected by you)”.",
            "“Use the store’s words again” brings back, at once, the title, author and series the book had before your correction. From then on the store’s list may change them again.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "What it is about", text: [
            "When you open a book’s page, the app asks Apple Books and Open Library, once, what the book is about. If a catalogue has the publisher’s description, the page shows it under “What it is about”, with “From the publisher, as a catalogue lists it.”",
            "A long text is cut to five lines, with “More” and “Less” under it.",
            "Only the title and the author are sent. The answer is kept in your shelf file. When nothing is found, the app asks again after 30 days. When there is no connection, it asks again another time.",
            "Not yet tried on your real books.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Series length by hand", text: [
            "On a series shelf's page tap “Set length” and type how many books the series has. Cancel closes without saving. Save keeps it.",
            "The heading then reads, for example, “3 of 7 · missing 2, 4, 6 and 7”.",
            "Your number stays even when a public catalogue says something else. An empty field takes your number away again.",
            "“Set length” is also on the series’ page (see The series’ page).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Where to buy", text: [
            "A book on your Wish list has a block “Where to buy”.",
            "It shows Apple Books' price in Swedish kronor when Apple Books shows one. Otherwise it says Apple Books shows no price. When Apple lists several editions, the first one that is not free counts.",
            "Two links, “Look on Kobo” and “Look on Kindle”, open those stores' search pages in your browser. Kobo and Kindle do not let an app read their prices.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Lock-free books and sending", text: [
            "A book is marked lock-free only when the app is sure. In this version that means your own files. A store book is never marked lock-free, even if its publisher sells it without a lock.",
            "On the Mac, a lock-free book has three buttons. Open in Apple Books opens the file in Books.",
            "Send to Kobo copies the file into the Dropbox folder Apps › Rakuten Kobo. A Kobo Libra Colour picks it up over Wi-Fi.",
            "Send to Kindle puts a copy in Downloads and opens Amazon's Send to Kindle page. There you choose that file.",
            "Some of your own EPUB books are kept by Apple Books as a folder, not as one file. Before 0.5 the app refused to send them (“opened-up folder”). Now Send to Kobo and Send to Kindle first pack such a book into one proper .epub file. Apple's own extra files (names starting with iTunes) are left out of the packed book. The original is only read, never changed.",
            "On iPhone and iPad the page says that sending is done on the Mac.",
            "Sending has not yet been proven from start to end.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Wish list", text: [
            "Books you want but do not own. Add them with Add a book, from a search that found nothing, from a barcode scan, from a series page, or from Share on iPhone and iPad.",
            "When a wished book later turns up in a store's list, it moves from the Wish list to the shelf by itself.",
            "A part of a series that you do not own can be put on the wish list with one tap on the series’ page (see The series’ page).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Settings", text: [
            "Open Settings with the cog button. It holds How it works, What's new, three choices under “Your shelf”, the lists and the version line.",
            "Under “Your shelf”: “Choose your shelves” (see Choose your shelves), “Kinds of notes” (see Reading notes) and “What changed” (see What changed).",
            "For each store the lists show how many books were found and when. Refresh reads them again. Import Kindle list takes in your Kindle library: Amazon's page saved as a PDF, or a CSV or JSON file.",
            "“Save the shelf as a list” has two buttons. “PDF” makes a printable list: title, author, series, stores, status and stars, with the wishes last. “Table” makes a file that a spreadsheet opens, with every column: title, author, series, number, stores, kind, language, status, finished, stars, note, bought and lock-free.",
            "Done closes Settings.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Your data and safety", text: [
            "The shelf is one file in the app's iCloud Drive folder. Mac, iPhone and iPad share it.",
            "Your own choices (reading status, wishes) are merged book by book. The newer change wins.",
            "A safety copy is kept on each device once a day, 14 days back.",
            "Since version 0.3 the shelf file has a new format (2), because it also holds your stars, notes, gave-up books and what the catalogues said about series.",
            "A device that still has version 0.2 shows the shelf but changes nothing until you update it. This keeps the new things from being lost.",
            "Since version 0.5 the shelf file has a newer format (3), because it also holds the Up next list, your reading goals, each book's language and finishing day, and a series length you typed. A device that still has version 0.4 shows the shelf but changes nothing until you update it to 0.5.",
            "Since version 0.6 the shelf file has a newer format (4), because it also holds your tags, reading notes and their kinds, the filters you kept as shelves, the choice and order of your shelves, details you corrected by hand, what a book is about, and what changed with each read of a store’s list. A device that still has version 0.5 shows the shelf but changes nothing until you update it to 0.6.",
            "Between devices: your reading notes and your kept shelves are merged one by one, and the newer change of each wins. A note or shelf you removed stays removed. The kinds of notes, and your choice and order of shelves, are kept as a whole: the newer one wins whole. The lists of what changed are put together.",
            "The Up next list and your goals are kept as a whole, each with the time of its last change. If two devices changed one, the newer one wins whole.",
            "The list for the device's own search stays on the device. Nothing is sent anywhere for it.",
            "What leaves your devices: for covers, series and prices, a book's title and author are sent to public book catalogues: Apple Books (iTunes Search), Open Library and Wikidata. For a barcode scan, the number is sent to Open Library and, if needed, Apple Books. Nothing else is sent, and never your whole list.",
            "Since 0.6, when you open a book’s page, its title and author are also sent to Apple Books and, if needed, Open Library, to ask what the book is about. Nothing else is sent. The answer is kept in your shelf file, so no device asks twice. When nothing is found, the app asks again after 30 days.",
            "Your reading notes, tags and kept shelves are never sent anywhere. Speaking a note and reading the words from a photo of a page happen on the device itself where the device can do so (see Reading notes).",
            "Cover pictures are also fetched from the stores' picture servers, as before.",
            "Kobo and Kindle prices are not asked for. Those two links open in your browser.",
            "The app asks a series catalogue once per series, and again after 60 days. A catalogue does not know every book. When it knows nothing, the app shows what it showed before.",
        ]),
    ]

    static let news: [Release] = [
        Release(version: "0.6", date: "18 November 2026", items: [
            "New: the big filter. A funnel button on the main screen (top left on iPhone and iPad, in the top bar on the Mac) opens one page of choices: Store, Kind, Language, Status, Your stars, Bought, Finished, Tags and Lock-free. Inside one group one choice is enough; across groups all must fit. The big button counts as you tap (“Show 42 books”). Wishes only come along when you choose Wish. Before, you could narrow down one shelf at a time, by store, kind, language and order.",
            "New: the result page. It shows the books that fit as covers, the choices in words and how many fit. “Change” goes back to the choices, and “Keep” keeps the filter as a shelf.",
            "New: shelves of your own. Keep a filter as a shelf and give it a name. It stands on the main screen, near the top, and is always up to date. On its page, “Change” lets you change the name and the choices, or “Remove this shelf” (the books stay on your shelf).",
            "New: the author’s page. Tap the author’s name on a book’s page to see all their books, the ones you own and the ones you wished for.",
            "New: the series’ page. Tap the series line on a book’s page, or “All parts” on a series shelf’s page. It shows every part in order: owned (with its status), wished and missing. A missing part goes on your wish list with one tap on “Wish”. “Set length” is there too.",
            "New: your own tags. On a book’s page, “Add” or “Edit” under Tags. Tap a tag to see every book that carries it. The search finds tags, and the big filter has them.",
            "New: Settings › Choose your shelves. Tick to show or hide a shelf, use the arrows for the order. All series shelves move together. “Back to the usual order” starts again. A hidden shelf keeps its books. Before, the shelves always stood in one fixed order, and only the Wish list could not be left out. Now it can be hidden too.",
            "New: “Surprise me” on the “What next?” card. It picks a book by chance among the books you own and have not started. “Another” takes you back to the ideas.",
            "New: progress on the covers. A started book carries a thin bar along the bottom of its cover, and its page says, for example, “42 % read”. This is the percent the store reports, read on the Mac. Apple Books and Kobo report it; Kindle does not.",
            "New: correct a book. At the bottom of a book’s page, “Correct title, author or series” has four fields: Title, Author, Series and Number in the series. After you save, the store’s list no longer changes them for that book. “Use the store’s words again” brings back, at once, the title, author and series from before your correction. A book you corrected is still known as the same book when another store lists it under its old title.",
            "New: what a book is about. A book’s page shows the publisher’s description, asked from Apple Books and Open Library when you open the page. Only the title and the author are sent. A long text is cut with “More” and “Less”. When nothing is found, the app asks again after 30 days.",
            "New: what changed. Settings › What changed lists the books that came and the books that left with each read of a store’s list, the newest read first (the last 40 reads). A small line at the bottom of the main screen shows the latest read, for example “Kobo: 3 came, 1 left · 18 Nov”. Reads are noted from 0.6 on. A store’s very first read is not noted, and neither is a read that changed no book.",
            "New: reading notes. The pen button at the right end of the “Reading notes” line on the main screen starts a note for the book you are reading now (you can change the book). Kinds: Quote, Thought, To do and Word to look up. Add your own kinds, rename them or remove them in Settings › Kinds of notes; the notes of a removed kind stand under “Other”.",
            "New, three ways to write a note: Type; Speak (the device asks once for the microphone and for speech, and listens on the device itself where it can); and Photo of the page (the camera reads the lines and you tick the ones to keep; on the Mac you choose a picture file). A field takes the page.",
            "New: the notes show on the book’s page and in the notebook. The line “Reading notes · 7” under “What next?” opens it. It holds all notes, the newest first, with the kinds on top to narrow them down. Tap a note to change it, share it or remove it.",
            "New: share a note. One note goes out by Share to any app, for example PARAGON. “Send all notes as one page” on a book’s page sends every note of the book as one tidy page, kind by kind.",
            "Changed: the main screen. The order of the shelves can now be your own. The funnel stands at the top left on iPhone and iPad, so the four buttons on the right stay where they were; on the Mac it stands in the top bar. Under the “What next?” card the line “Reading notes” leads to the notebook and carries the pen for a new note, and a small line at the very bottom leads to what changed.",
            "Changed: a book’s page. New parts: Tags, Reading notes and What it is about. The author’s name and the series line are now taps. At the bottom stands “Correct title, author or series”. Before, the author and the series were plain words.",
            "Changed: the search finds your tags as well as titles, authors, series and your own notes.",
            "Changed: Settings has a new part, “Your shelf”, with Choose your shelves, Kinds of notes and What changed.",
            "Changed for safety: the shelf file has a new format (4). A device that still has version 0.5 shows the shelf but changes nothing until it is updated to 0.6. This keeps the new things from being lost. Your reading notes and kept shelves are merged one by one between devices; the newer change wins.",
            "What leaves your devices, new in 0.6: a book’s title and author are sent to Apple Books and Open Library to ask what the book is about. Your notes, tags and shelves are never sent anywhere.",
            "Not tried yet: Speak, Photo of the page and sharing a note to another app on a real device, and the description lookup on your real books.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.5", date: "17 November 2026", items: [
            "New: Up next. On the page of a book you own and have not started there is a button “Add to Up next” (later “Take off Up next”). The main screen has a new shelf “Up next · N” (indigo) right under Reading now. On that shelf's page, “Change order” opens a page where you move books up or down or take them off, with Cancel and Save. A book you start or finish leaves the list by itself. Before, you had no list of your own.",
            "Changed: “What next?” now offers your Up next books first, in your order (“First on your Up next list”), and only then its own ideas.",
            "New: the list travels between your devices. If two devices changed it, the newer list wins, whole.",
            "New: Your reading. A small button in the top bar of the main screen opens “Your reading”, with two parts: “Year” and “Numbers”.",
            "New, Year: for each year a heading such as “2026 · 8 books”, with the books you finished that year (latest first), your stars and the day you finished them. “Set goal” asks how many books you want to finish this year. Then a line such as “8 of 24 · 2 behind”, “ahead”, “on track” or “goal reached” shows how you stand. A goal holds for the following years until you change it. Empty or 0 means no goal.",
            "New: the day a book was finished. It is today when you mark a book Finished, and the day the store last saw it open when a store reports it finished. Books you finished before 0.5 count on the day they were last opened. On a finished book's page you can correct the day. Finished books whose day nobody knows stand under “Day not known”.",
            "New, Numbers: how many books you own, how many wishes, how many are lock-free and how many you bought twice. Bars by store, by kind, by reading status, by language, books bought per year and books finished per year. Your average stars.",
            "New: shelf by language. Every book now carries its language. Apple Books and Kobo tell it for most books. Kindle does not. Where no store tells, the app makes a careful guess from the title: only Swedish, German or English, and only when it is sure. Titles shorter than 12 letters get no guess. The letter å means Swedish, the letter ß means German.",
            "New: a book's page shows “Language” and lets you change it. What you set by hand is never overwritten. On a shelf's page the sort and filter control has a new choice “Language”.",
            "New on iPhone and iPad: the Home Screen widget “Reading”. Small: the book you are reading now. Medium: up to two books you are reading now and one “What next?” idea with its reason. A tap opens that book's page. It refreshes when the shelf changes and at least every 6 hours. The widget fetches the covers itself. Without a cover it shows the same coloured title tile as in the app. Not on the Mac.",
            "New: the search of your iPhone, iPad and Mac finds your books. Type a title or an author. The result says “You own this · Kobo, Kindle” or “On your wish list”, and a tap opens the book's page. The list it searches is kept on the device only. Nothing is sent anywhere.",
            "Fixed: sending your own EPUB books that Apple Books keeps as folders. Before 0.5 the app refused them (“opened-up folder”). Now, on the Mac, Send to Kobo and Send to Kindle first pack such a book into one proper .epub file. Apple's own extra files (names starting with iTunes) are left out of the packed book. The original is only read, never changed.",
            "New: a series' length by hand. On a series shelf's page, “Set length” asks how many books the series has, with Cancel and Save. The heading then reads, for example, “3 of 7 · missing 2, 4, 6 and 7”. Your number stays even when a catalogue says something else. An empty field takes your number away again.",
            "New: Settings › “Save the shelf as a list”, with two buttons. “PDF” makes a printable list: title, author, series, stores, status and stars, with the wishes last. “Table” makes a file that a spreadsheet opens, with every column: title, author, series, number, stores, kind, language, status, finished, stars, note, bought and lock-free.",
            "Changed for safety: the shelf file has a new format (3). A device that still has version 0.4 shows the shelf but changes nothing until it is updated to 0.5. This keeps the new things from being lost.",
            "Behind the scenes: the app's code is now public on GitHub, with automatic tests on every change. No real book titles are in it. The sample and the tests use made-up books.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.4", date: "17 November 2026", items: [
            "Changed on iPad and Mac: the shelves and the book now stand side by side. Before, iPad and Mac showed the iPhone screen stretched: a tap on a cover replaced the shelves with the book's page, and Back brought the shelves back. Now, when the window is wide (760 points or more), the screen has two parts. Left: the shelves exactly as before. Right: the page of the book you tapped, without leaving the shelves.",
            "New: tap another cover and the right part changes to that book. The chosen cover has a purple frame. When the app opens, the first book of Reading now is shown on the right. If there is none, the right part says “Tap a cover and the book shows here.”",
            "Changed: a tap on a shelf's heading still opens that shelf's page (grid with sort and filter) on the left. A tap on a cover there also shows the book on the right. “Next in the series” on the right shows the next book on the right.",
            "Unchanged: a narrow window (under 760 points, such as a small Mac window or an iPad in a narrow split) and the iPhone work exactly as before. Search, Add a book, Scan and Settings are unchanged, and a book opened from Search still opens inside the Search sheet.",
            "Not changed: the shelf file (still format 2) and the lookups. Not tried yet: the two parts on a real iPad.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.3", date: "17 November 2026", items: [
            "New: a “What next?” card above the shelves. Before, nothing suggested a book. Now it gives one idea: the next book of a series whose earlier book you finished, then your newest book, then the ones that have waited longest. “Another” shows the next idea.",
            "New: the shelf “Set aside”. Before, books you started but did not open for a long time stood under Not read yet (72 such books on your shelf). Now books you have not opened in the 90 days before your latest reading stand under Set aside, and Not read yet holds only books you have not started.",
            "New: on the page of a started book, “Gave up on it” moves it from Set aside to the new shelf “Gave up”. “Pick it up again” undoes that.",
            "New: the shelf “Bought twice”: every book you own to read, or to listen to, in two stores. Today that is 7 books on your shelf, 3 of them Kindle plus Apple Books. Your own files do not count.",
            "New: your own stars (1 to 5) and one note for each book, on its page under “Your stars and note”, with Edit, Cancel and Save. Search also finds words in your notes. Before, there was nowhere to write about a book.",
            "New: “Next in the series” on a book's page. It shows the next book with its cover when you own it, its name when a public catalogue knows it (with “Add to wish list”), or only its number.",
            "Changed: a series shelf now says “3 of 7 · missing 2 and 4” when a public catalogue knows how long the series is. Before, it said only “3 owned · missing 2” and knew gaps only below your highest number. When the catalogue does not know the series, the note stays as before. The app asks once per series, and again after 60 days. On your shelf today the catalogue (Wikidata) knows 3 of your 11 series: Fjällbacka (12 books), Millennium (3) and Utvandrarserien (4). It looks a series up by one of your books' titles, in English, Swedish or German; publishers' collections such as “Everyone Can Code” are not series it knows.",
            "New: cover pictures for books that came without one: the 25 Kindle books from your list, books you added by hand, and wishes. The app asks a public catalogue once per book, by title and author. When nothing fits, the coloured title tile stays.",
            "New: sort and filter on a shelf's page (tap the shelf's heading): one store, to read or to listen, and the order (shelf order, by title, by author, newest first). A line says, for example, “Showing 12 of 340.”",
            "New on iPhone and iPad: the Share check. In Safari, on a book's page, choose Share › AMS Reading. It says “You own this” with the store, “Already on your wish list”, or “Not on your shelf” with the title and author to check and the buttons Save to wish list and Cancel. Not on the Mac. Not yet tried on a real iPhone.",
            "New on iPhone and iPad: “Scan a barcode” under the add button. The camera reads the barcode of a printed book, or you type the number under it. The app says that you own it, that it is on your wish list, or that it is not on your shelf (then “Add to wish list”). On the Mac the add button adds a book by hand, as before. Not yet tried on a real iPhone.",
            "New: wish-list prices. A wish's page has “Where to buy”: Apple Books' price in Swedish kronor when Apple shows one, and two links that open the Kobo and Kindle search pages in your browser. Those two stores do not let an app read their prices.",
            "New: Settings › Import Kindle list now takes Amazon's page “Manage Your Content and Devices” saved as a PDF, straight from the file. Before, it took only a CSV or JSON file. It tells you when Amazon showed only part of your list (for example 25 of 33), so you can save the next page too. Books that Amazon marks READ come in as Finished.",
            "Fixed: a book that a list file brings again (same title, same store) no longer gets a second copy of that store.",
            "Changed for safety: the shelf file has a new format (2). A device that still has version 0.2 shows the shelf but changes nothing until it is updated.",
            "What leaves your devices: for covers, series and prices, a book's title and author (or the barcode number) are sent to public book catalogues: Apple Books (iTunes Search), Open Library and Wikidata. Nothing else is sent, and never your whole list.",
            "Not tried yet: the Share check and the camera scan on a real iPhone, the Open buttons on iPhone, and sending lock-free books.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.2", date: "17 November 2026", items: [
            "Fixed: the Kindle list showed things that are not your books. Before: 16 Kindle “books” on the shelf. What they really were: 1 book you bought, 3 dictionaries that come built into Kindle, and 12 nameless entries whose title was only a row of dashes (Kindle's built-in dictionaries for other languages). Now: built-in entries are left out, meaning anything in Kindle's list without a purchase day and anything whose title has no letters or numbers. On your Mac that leaves 1 Kindle book on the shelf (were 16): the one you bought.",
            "New rule: when a store's list later marks a book on your shelf as a free sample or a built-in item, the book leaves the shelf by itself at the next read. Before, it stayed.",
            "Changed: Settings now says honestly how the Kindle count comes about. It counts the books on the shelf that came from Kindle, not the size of your Kindle library.",
            "Still open: the Kindle app on the Mac does not hold your whole Kindle library, and Amazon's website does not let a helper read it. Your full Kindle list comes in through Settings › Import Kindle list or Add a book.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.1", date: "17 November 2026", items: [
            "New: the first version. One shelf for your books from Rakuten Kobo, Apple Books and Amazon Kindle, and for your own files (EPUB and PDF), on Mac, iPhone and iPad.",
            "New: the shelf holds bought ebooks, audiobooks and your own files. Free samples are left out.",
            "New: the Mac reads your Apple Books list from the Books app. On 17 November 2026 it found 299 ebooks, 78 audiobooks and 22 PDFs.",
            "New: the Mac reads your Kobo list from Kobo's Mac app. Not yet checked against your real Kobo list.",
            "New: Kindle books come in three ways: new purchases from the Kindle app on the Mac, a one-time list file of your whole library (Settings › Import Kindle list), and by hand.",
            "New: the lists are read each time the app opens on the Mac, and when you press Refresh in Settings. Settings shows for each store how many books were found and when.",
            "New: iPhone and iPad show the same shelf through iCloud.",
            "New: a book that leaves Apple Books or Kobo leaves your shelf at the next read. An empty list removes nothing. Kindle books are never removed by themselves.",
            "New: the same book in two stores is one book with two store marks. Matching ignores capital letters, accents, subtitles after a colon and a leading “The”.",
            "New: “Owned twice” when you own the same kind (ebook or audiobook) in two stores.",
            "New: the main screen is a stack of shelves with rows of covers that scroll sideways: Reading now, Not read yet (newest first, with the count), one shelf for each series, Lock-free, Audiobooks, Finished and Wish list.",
            "New: series shelves show a note such as “3 owned · missing 2 and 4”.",
            "New: tap a shelf's heading to see all its books in a grid.",
            "New: small coloured dots under each cover show the stores, with an open-lock mark and a headphones mark. A book without a cover shows a coloured tile with its title.",
            "New: search by title, author or series, to check whether you already own a book before you buy it. If nothing matches, you can add it with one button.",
            "New: Add a book, with a choice of Wish list, Kindle, Kobo, Apple Books or Own file. It warns you if you already own the title.",
            "New: a book's page with cover, details, reading status and one Open button for each store.",
            "New: reading status Not read, Reading and Finished. It starts from the store, and your own choice stays once you set it.",
            "New: Remove from shelf for wishes, books added by hand and Kindle books. It asks first.",
            "New: your own files are marked lock-free. On the Mac they have Open in Apple Books, Send to Kobo and Send to Kindle buttons.",
            "New: a wished book moves to the shelf by itself when a store's list brings it.",
            "New: Settings with How it works, What's new, the lists and the version line.",
            "New: the shelf is one file in iCloud Drive. A safety copy is kept on each device once a day, 14 days back. An older app meeting a newer shelf file shows it but changes nothing.",
            "New: hand-drawn icons, a compact look, headings in colour, full-colour main buttons, day and night.",
            "Not tried yet: Open for Apple Books and Kobo, opening from iPhone and iPad, and all three Send buttons from start to end.",
        ]),
    ]
}
